From 19f838c0728c602a91c1c63b9062567e61ebf161 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Myers <jmyers2010@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 00:21:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Share one keyboard and mouse across machines

Omarchy has no answer to Universal Control or Mouse Without Borders: put a
second machine on the desk and you need a second keyboard for it. The manual's
own "Coming From Mac or Windows" translation table has a row for AirDrop and
nothing for this.

Wire up Lan Mouse, which is in extra and speaks the two wlroots protocols
Hyprland already implements -- layer-shell to capture the pointer at a screen
edge, wlr-virtual-pointer and virtual-keyboard to replay it on the other side.
Traffic is DTLS encrypted and machines authorize each other by certificate
fingerprint.

  omarchy install service lan-mouse    installs it, starts it with the
                                       graphical session, opens UDP 4242 for
                                       private LANs and Tailscale
  omarchy setup input sharing          pairs a machine, from the menu or the
                                       command line

Two rough edges in lan-mouse's own CLI are smoothed over here, because both sit
right on the path a first-time user walks:

- add-client takes no position, so a new client lands wherever lan-mouse
  defaults and has to be moved afterwards. `add` does both, and works out which
  client is new by diffing the list around the call -- ids are neither
  sequential nor sorted, so neither the highest nor the last is reliable.

- there is no command to read your own fingerprint back out, though pairing
  needs it on the other machine. `fingerprint` derives it from the certificate
  the daemon writes on first run, the same SHA-256 over the DER that lan-mouse
  compares against.

The unit only lands in the user's config when the system has none, so a
packaged unit is never shadowed by a stale copy.
